results.abstract:
The use of indentation testing for plastic strain estimations in wrought aluminum alloys is not inherently novel. However, a well-documented and quantified hardness-strain correlation capable of characterizing strain accumulation in complex forming operations, such as spin and flow forming, is needed. Given the microstructure dependencies on plastic strain (composition, grain structure, texture), it is essential to conduct plastic strain accumulation calibration on each alloy and temper of interest. The experimental approach included systematic rolling reductions from 15% to 91% coupled with comprehensive microhardness testing and finite element validation using the DEFORM® simulation suite. The outcome was an empirical power-law relationship for converting Vickers indentation measurements to equivalent plastic strain values, unique to Al 6061-O plate;  

                                                 ϵ_p= 4.4836H^6.3161-0.08 

Statistical analysis determined the precision of the empirical relationship using the simulation results, analytic solutions, and variance within the hardness data. It was established that the empirical relationship is within 7.55% of the measured hardness value, and within 18.6% of model simulations. It is anticipated that the correlation may be applied to more complex strain fields by exploiting the analytic plane strain states established for rolled material.
